content.text:
I've set up a Rust library crate called `platform` in `/work`. It uses the [`cfg-if`](https://crates.io/crates/cfg-if) crate, which lets you write platform checks as a single `if / else if / else` chain instead of many separate `#[cfg]` attributes. It builds, both tests pass, and `cargo clippy` with warnings treated as errors is clean. I only checked this on Linux, since that's the only target installed.

**Layout**
```
Cargo.toml          # edition 2024, depends on cfg-if 1.0
src/lib.rs          # public API, with no #[cfg] anywhere
src/sys/mod.rs      # the one place that chooses a platform
src/sys/{windows,macos,linux,wasm,unix}.rs   # one file per platform
```

**How it works.** All platform selection happens in `src/sys/mod.rs`:

```rust
cfg_if::cfg_if! {
    if #[cfg(windows)] {
        mod windows;
        pub use self::windows::*;
    } else if #[cfg(target_os = "macos")] {
        ...
    } else if #[cfg(unix)] {
        mod unix;              // fallback for BSDs, illumos, etc.
        pub use self::unix::*;
    } else {
        compile_error!("unsupported target platform; add a backend in src/sys/");
    }
}
```

- **First match wins.** The chain stops at the first branch that matches, so specific targets (`macos`, `linux`) must come before broad ones (`unix`). There's a comment in the file saying this.
- **Each platform file defines the same items.** The rest of the crate just calls `sys::...` and never needs its own `#[cfg]` checks. As starter content, each file has two constants: a platform name and the separator used in `PATH`.
- **Unsupported targets fail at compile time.** The final `else` stops the build with a clear error instead of producing a crate with missing items.

**Adding a platform.** Create `src/sys/<name>.rs` with the same items as the others, then add a branch to the chain above the broader fallback it would otherwise fall into.

**Not done yet:**
- **Other targets aren't checked.** To cover them, install them with `rustup target add x86_64-pc-windows-msvc aarch64-apple-darwin wasm32-unknown-unknown` and run `cargo check --target <triple>` for each, ideally in CI.
- **Nothing is committed.** You're on `master` and nothing is committed yet; I left that for you.

